From: Timofey Maykov <itnitro@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] spi: qpic-snand: fall back to 4 bit ECC when 8 bit does not fit the OOB

The Xiaomi BE7000 ships with a 128 byte OOB GigaDevice SPI NAND and, on
newer boards, a 64 byte OOB ESMT F50D1G41LB. The device tree asks for 8
bit ECC, which needs 532 byte codewords: four of them do not fit a 2048
+ 64 byte page, so every read of the ESMT part returned -EBADMSG and UBI
could not attach. The vendor firmware uses 4 bit ECC on that part. Fall
back to 4 bits when the configured 8 bit layout does not fit; parts with
enough OOB keep 8 bits and the data already written with it.

Signed-off-by: Timofey Maykov <itnitro@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/spi/spi-qpic-snand.c
+++ b/drivers/spi/spi-qpic-snand.c
@@ -274,6 +274,21 @@
 		ecc_cfg->strength = 4;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * An 8 bit codeword (516 data bytes, 13 parity, 2 spare and the bad
+	 * block byte) needs 2 KiB pages with 128 bytes of OOB. Boards that
+	 * ship with either flash, such as the Xiaomi BE7000, describe 8 bits
+	 * in the device tree; on a 64 byte OOB part 8 bits cannot fit and the
+	 * vendor firmware uses 4 bits, so do the same.
+	 */
+	if (ecc_cfg->strength == 8 &&
+	    cwperpage * (516 + 13 + 2 + 1) > mtd->writesize + mtd->oobsize) {
+		dev_info(snandc->dev,
+			 "8 bit ECC does not fit %u bytes of OOB, using 4 bit\n",
+			 mtd->oobsize);
+		ecc_cfg->strength = 4;
+	}
+
 	if (ecc_cfg->step_size != NANDC_STEP_SIZE) {
 		dev_err(snandc->dev,
 			"only %u bytes ECC step size is supported\n",
